Measured on 40 requests. The question is what a sampled dashboard sees.

    kept                     p50                     p95                     p99
                    truth 3078ms            truth 8033ms            truth 9657ms
   100%             3078-3078ms             8033-8033ms             9657-9657ms
    50%             3012-3490ms             4939-8332ms             6586-9657ms
    25%             2870-3490ms             4667-9657ms             4667-9657ms
    10%             2900-5798ms             3078-9657ms             3044-9657ms
     5%             2590-4667ms             2870-8332ms             2870-8332ms

At one trace in ten, the median is still readable — 2900 to 5798ms against a
true 3078 — and p99 has become a wide guess (3044 to 9657ms).

That asymmetry is the whole of sampling policy. A median is a statement about
the middle of the distribution and survives thinning; a p99 is a statement
about the rarest one in a hundred, and thinning is exactly the operation that
removes it.

So do not sample uniformly. Sample the way the questions differ:

  keep everything that failed        errors are rare and are the ones you
                                     will be asked about
  keep everything that was slow      a tail you have thrown away cannot be
                                     investigated, only counted
  keep everything a user marked      feedback without its trace is an opinion
  sample the boring middle hard      a p50 needs far fewer traces than a p99

This is tail-based sampling, and the name is the mechanism: the decision is
made when the trace finishes and its duration and status are known, not when
it starts. Head-based sampling — decide at the first span — is cheaper and
throws away precisely the traces you wanted.
